WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
Pond S2 has 20 qPCR wells.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.660
Every one is negative.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:11.620
Is the species gone?

4
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.460
Not yet. A false negative is a miss.

5
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.180
The organism or its DNA is present, and nothing amplifies.

6
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.920
To see why, follow the DNA from the pond to the well.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:29.590
Think of the survey as three nets, each with holes.

8
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.570
First, DNA must be in the water you scoop.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.430
We set that chance at 0.6.

10
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.930
Second, the extraction must capture it.

11
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.310
In a wild pig study in central Texas, that chance was 0.49.

12
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.310
Third, a qPCR run must amplify it.

13
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.690
That chance was 0.38.

14
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.170
We borrow both as illustrative estimates from one species and one region.

15
00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.210
Each net can let the signal slip through.

16
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.533
Start with the lab.

17
00:01:03.513 --> 00:01:06.753
One run misses with probability 0.62.

18
00:01:07.813 --> 00:01:12.233
All five runs miss with probability 0.092.

19
00:01:12.893 --> 00:01:18.353
So five runs amplify captured DNA with probability 0.908.

20
00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:24.993
Add the capture step, and two extractions detect with probability 0.692.

21
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:34.053
Now the scoop. 0.6 times 0.692 gives 0.415.

22
00:01:35.413 --> 00:01:39.253
That is the chance one water sample detects an occupied pond.

23
00:01:40.593 --> 00:01:42.033
Could more wells fix it?

24
00:01:42.813 --> 00:01:46.313
Even perfect lab work gives no more than 0.6.

25
00:01:47.193 --> 00:01:51.133
If the scoop held no DNA, no number of runs will find it.

26
00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:58.177
Only another independent water sample attacks the 40% chance that the scoop holds

27
00:01:58.177 --> 00:02:05.457
no DNA. Two samples both miss an occupied pond with probability 0.342.

28
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:09.837
Start from a prior occupancy of 0.2.

29
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:15.217
Bayes' rule then gives 7.9% that the pond is still occupied.

30
00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:20.517
The team stops only at 5% or below, so it samples again.

31
00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:25.637
A third negative sample gives 4.8%, just under the line.

32
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.850
Now count the 20 wells as 20 independent tries.

33
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.170
That is pseudoreplication.

34
00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:39.410
It gives 2.3%, and you would stop on an error in the model, not on evidence.

35
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.570
Even the honest numbers rest on choices.

36
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.330
The 0.6, the prior, and the 5% are ours.

37
00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.790
Changing them can change the decision.

38
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.390
Wells from one sample share its scoop.

39
00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.630
Only new water samples count as new tries.
